## TL;DR  

* **You didn’t miss a step** – the APIs you were using (`map.setFog()`, the free-camera helpers, etc.) simply **don’t exist in MapLibre**.  
* MapLibre replaces those Mapbox-v2 features with **different APIs** (`map.setSky()`, `map.calculateCameraOptionsFromCameraLngLatAltRotation()` / `jumpTo`/`easeTo`/`flyTo`).  
* The rest of your migration (package, namespace, token, style URL, plugins, etc.) is already correct; you just need to swap the **v2-only calls for their MapLibre equivalents**.

Below is a concise “what-to-do” guide, plus full code snippets, a checklist, and pointers to the official docs so you can finish the migration without hunting down missing methods.

---

## 1. Why those calls are broken

| Mapbox GL JS v2 feature | MapLibre GL JS status |
|--------------------------|-----------------------|
| `map.setFog({...})` (and the `fog` root-level style object) | **Not implemented** in any MapLibre version. |
| `map.getFreeCameraOptions()` / `map.setFreeCameraOptions()` (FreeCameraOptions class) | **Removed** – MapLibre never added these methods. |
| Any other v2-only methods (e.g. `map.setTerrain({exaggeration: …})` that rely on the `terrain` source) | Still works **if you use a `terrain` source**; only the *fog* and *free-camera* APIs are missing. |

MapLibre’s development forked from Mapbox GL JS v1.13, **before** those v2 methods were added. The library has its own way to achieve the same visual effect, but the method names and style spec sections are different.

---

## 2. Re-creating the “fog” effect with **Sky**

### 2.1. What changed in the style spec

* **Mapbox**: `fog` object inside the style JSON (`"fog": { "color": "#fff", "high-color": "#fff", … }`) and the runtime method `map.setFog()`.
* **MapLibre**: `sky` object (`"sky": { "sky-color": "#199EF3", "horizon-color": "#fff", "fog-color": "#000", … }`) and the runtime method `map.setSky()`.

> **Tip:** The `sky` spec is still marked *experimental* but works in all current releases (v6+). If you only need a simple atmospheric haze, the default `sky` values are enough.

### 2.2. Example conversion

```js
// ---- Mapbox (v2) ----
map.setFog({
  "color": "white",
  "high-color": "#add8e6",
  "space-color": "#d8f2ff",
  "horizon-blend": 0.3
});
```

```js
// ---- MapLibre (v6) ----
map.setSky({
  // sky-color = color of the dome (above the horizon)
  "sky-color": "#ffffff",
  // horizon-color = color at the horizon line
  "horizon-color": "#add8e6",
  // fog-color = colour of the fog that blends into the ground
  "fog-color": "#d8f2ff",
  // blend factors – 0-1 (higher = more fog)
  "fog-ground-blend": 0.5,
  "horizon-fog-blend": 0.3,
  // optional extra blends (keep defaults if you don’t need them)
  "sky-horizon-blend": 0.5,
  "atmosphere-blend": 1.0
});
```

#### Adding `sky` to a style JSON (if you prefer static style)

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": { … },
  "layers": [ … ],
  "sky": {
    "type": "sky",
    "sky-type": "atmosphere",
    "sky-color": "#ffffff",
    "horizon-color": "#add8e6",
    "fog-color": "#d8f2ff",
    "fog-ground-blend": 0.5,
    "horizon-fog-blend": 0.3
  }
}
```

*If you already have a style file, just add the `sky` object at the top level (outside `layers`).*

---

## 3. Re-creating the “free-camera” API

### 3.1. What you lose

* `map.getFreeCameraOptions()` – returns an object with `center`, `zoom`, `pitch`, `bearing`, `altitude`, `roll`.
* `map.setFreeCameraOptions(opts)` – sets the camera directly from that object.

### 3.2. MapLibre’s replacement workflow

1. **Calculate a `CameraOptions` object** from the desired lng/lat/alt/rotation using the helper method `map.calculateCameraOptionsFromCameraLngLatAltRotation()`.  
2. **Apply the options** with any of the standard camera movement methods (`jumpTo`, `easeTo`, `flyTo`).

> **Why two steps?**  
> MapLibre’s camera model is the same as Mapbox’s (center, zoom, pitch, bearing) plus `elevation` (v3) and `roll` (v5). The helper does the math for you, then you feed the result to the familiar movement API.

### 3.3. Example conversion

```js
// ---- Mapbox (v2) ----
const opts = map.getFreeCameraOptions();   // {center, zoom, pitch, bearing, altitude, roll}
opts.center = [13.4, 52.5];
opts.altitude = 2000;      // metres above ground
opts.pitch = 60;
opts.bearing = 45;
map.setFreeCameraOptions(opts);
```

```js
// ---- MapLibre (v6) ----
/* 1️⃣  Build the target camera position */
const targetLngLat = [13.4, 52.5];
const targetAltitude = 2000;   // metres above ground (requires terrain/elevation source)
const targetBearing = 45;      // degrees clockwise from north
const targetPitch = 60;        // degrees
const targetRoll = 0;          // optional, v5+

/* 2️⃣  Convert to CameraOptions */
const camOpts = map.calculateCameraOptionsFromCameraLngLatAltRotation(
  targetLngLat,          // [lng, lat]
  targetAltitude,       // altitude in metres
  targetBearing,        // bearing
  targetPitch,          // pitch
  targetRoll            // roll (v5+)
);

/* 3️⃣  Move the camera – choose the animation you like */
map.jumpTo(camOpts);          // instant
// or
map.easeTo(camOpts, {duration: 1200}); // smooth
// or
map.flyTo(camOpts, {speed: 1.2});       // “fly” animation
```

#### Using the helper for a *look-at* (camera from point A looking at point B)

```js
// From Mapbox:
// map.setFreeCameraOptions({center: A, altitude: 500, bearing: 0, pitch: 45, roll: 0});
// map.lookAt(B);   // not a real Mapbox method, but conceptually you wanted to point at B

// MapLibre equivalent:
const from = [13.4, 52.5];          // camera position (lng, lat)
const fromAlt = 500;               // metres
const to = [13.45, 52.55];         // point you want to look at
const bearing = 0;
const pitch = 45;
const roll = 0;

// Helper that returns a CameraOptions that looks from `from` to `to`
const camOpts = map.calculateCameraOptionsFromTo(
  from, fromAlt,   // eye position
  to,   0          // target position (ground level altitude = 0)
);

/* Apply */
map.easeTo(camOpts);
```

> **Note:** `calculateCameraOptionsFromTo` existed in MapLibre v2.4.0 and is still present in v6. It’s handy when you have a *look-at* scenario.

---

## 4. Full Minimal Example (MapLibre v6)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – fog → sky & free-camera</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';

  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty', // any non-Mapbox style
    center: [-122.42, 37.78],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 45,
    bearing: 0,
    // optional: terrain source for altitude support
    // terrain: {source: 'terrain-source', exaggeration: 1}
  });

  // Add navigation controls (same API)
  map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());

  // Wait for the style to load before calling setSky / camera helpers
  map.on('load', () => {
    // ---- SKY (replaces fog) ----
    map.setSky({
      'sky-color': '#199EF3',
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
      'fog-color': '#cceeff',
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.4,
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.3,
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
      'atmosphere-blend': 1.0
    });

    // ---- FREE-CAMERA REPLACEMENT ----
    const camOpts = map.calculateCameraOptionsFromCameraLngLatAltRotation(
      [-122.42, 37.78],   // lng, lat
      1500,               // altitude in metres (requires terrain source)
      30,                 // bearing
      60,                 // pitch
      0                   // roll (optional)
    );
    map.easeTo(camOpts, {duration: 2000});
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Run the snippet in a modern browser (ES-module support). The map will load, show a blue-sky atmosphere, and smoothly lift the camera to 1.5 km altitude with a 60° pitch.*

